put document doc.00b1d791c652b7d601291cb1 { anchors = { specifications = 1 }, content = "# Specifications\n\nThe accepted GridSpec state under `refs/gridspec/projects/...` is authoritative.\n[`authority.gridspec`](authority.gridspec) is the reviewed incremental transaction for the\nnew authority model. [`cas-compilation.gridspec`](cas-compilation.gridspec) makes\nCAS materialization mandatory for all downstream consumers and records the\naccepted/proposal preview distinction. [`gridspec.txn`](gridspec.txn) is a\ngenerated text snapshot used for inspection and bootstrap recovery; regenerate\nit with:\n\n```sh\ngridspec view --format text --output specs/gridspec.txn\n```\n\nThe `.dot` and `.md` files in this directory are retained as legacy migration\ninputs and historical provenance. They are not edited as the source of truth.\nGenerate compact diagrams with `gridspec view --format dot`; use `--graph ID`\nfor one detailed graph. The checked-in\n[`gridspec-authority.dot`](../views/gridspec-authority.dot) is the generated\nview of the transactional authority and compilation boundary.\n", links = [{ Destination = "authority.gridspec", Line = 4 }, { Destination = "cas-compilation.gridspec", Line = 5 }, { Destination = "gridspec.txn", Line = 7 }, { Destination = "../views/gridspec-authority.dot", Line = 19 }], path = "specs/README.md", sha256 = "f6ad7889c018b11aa06101c72615e350f254ddab708b32fcc020984b1162cf55" }
put document doc.0cdcd21a4b8f0b4bac5b6663 { anchors = { bootstrap-and-future-generation = 1 }, content = "# Bootstrap and future generation\n\n[DOT source](architecture.dot)\n\nThe current Go implementation and independent unit/regression tests remain the\nrelease bootstrap authority. This self-specification is normative design input\nand a continuously checked consistency gate, but validating it with the same\nimplementation does not prove that implementation correct.\n\n`specdot conform` adds deterministic symbol, scenario, and semantic-trace\nevidence without claiming that self-validation is an independent proof. The\nsealed `specdot.ir/v1` artifact remains the only intended input to a future\n`specdot build`; generation and independently bootstrapped verification remain\ndeferred to that later milestone.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: decision.specdot.bootstrap\nkind: decision\nsummary: Separate present dogfooding guarantees from future self-hosted generation.\nmetadata:\n  current_bootstrap: Go implementation plus independent tests\n  self_spec_guarantee: internal consistency and reviewed behavioral intent\n  current_conformance: Go symbol, command, test, trace, coverage, and baseline evidence\n  future_generation_input: specdot.ir/v1\n```\n", links = [{ Destination = "architecture.dot", Line = 3 }], path = "specs/architecture.md", sha256 = "44fba94675b82f40f7bdaa0bbc2f569203fb6c0db2b2b6705f4e89afdf6e35c4" }
put document doc.149b55ceca116cd8290cec3d { anchors = { clean-conformance = 3, conformance-scenarios = 1 }, content = "# Conformance scenarios\n\n## Clean conformance\n\n[DOT source](scenario-conform-clean.dot)\n\nA clean conformance run vets the specification, resolves all declared Go\nobjects, observes both command and Go-test behavior, verifies required trace\nevents, classifies every active ID, meets its coverage floors, and preserves\nthe checked-in evidence baseline. It exits with status 0.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: scenario.specdot.conform.clean\nkind: scenario\nsummary: Reviewed implementation evidence agrees with the canonical specification.\n```\n", links = [{ Destination = "scenario-conform-clean.dot", Line = 5 }], path = "specs/conform-scenarios.md", sha256 = "21bc320045fd62cb30e7e2040e3fb9295f638e06c0bff13cc10cf3216bc74dc9" }
put document doc.1f0c593062c3d47798957d14 { anchors = { clean-validation = 3, configuration-failure = 46, deterministic-repeat = 76, ir-emission-failure = 61, required-inconclusive-check = 31, semantic-findings = 17, validation-scenarios = 1 }, content = "# Validation scenarios\n\n## Clean validation\n\n[DOT source](scenario-clean.dot)\n\nA complete local module under a finite-required profile produces a sealed\ndigest, an empty diagnostic report, and process status 0.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: scenario.specdot.clean\nkind: scenario\nsummary: A complete module validates without findings.\n```\n\n## Semantic findings\n\n[DOT source](scenario-findings.dot)\n\nSyntax, linking, semantic, or violated-property errors remain diagnostics. The\nreport is emitted deterministically and the process exits with status 1.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: scenario.specdot.findings\nkind: scenario\nsummary: Validation findings are reported with process status 1.\n```\n\n## Required inconclusive check\n\n[DOT source](scenario-required-inconclusive.dot)\n\nUnsupported history semantics or exhausted bounds yield an inconclusive check.\nWhen finite checking is required, that conclusion is an error and the process\nexits with status 1 after reporting it.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: scenario.specdot.required_inconclusive\nkind: scenario\nsummary: Required finite work cannot silently pass when proof is inconclusive.\n```\n\n## Configuration failure\n\n[DOT source](scenario-config-failure.dot)\n\nA missing manifest, malformed strict YAML, unknown profile, or discovery error\nis written to stderr and terminates with status 2 before a diagnostic report is\nconstructed.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: scenario.specdot.config_failure\nkind: scenario\nsummary: Usage and setup failures terminate before semantic validation.\n```\n\n## IR emission failure\n\n[DOT source](scenario-emit-failure.dot)\n\nIf a requested temporary write, sync, close, or rename fails, the prior target\nis retained, the error is written to stderr, and the process exits with status\n2 without claiming successful publication.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: scenario.specdot.emit_failure\nkind: scenario\nsummary: Failed IR publication preserves the previous artifact and fails closed.\n```\n\n## Deterministic repeat\n\n[DOT source](scenario-repeat.dot)\n\nTwo invocations over identical source bytes, configuration, profile, and\nsupported environment assumptions produce byte-identical normalized IR and the\nsame SHA-256 digest.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: scenario.specdot.repeat\nkind: scenario\nsummary: Identical validation inputs produce identical canonical IR.\n```\n", links = [{ Destination = "scenario-clean.dot", Line = 5 }, { Destination = "scenario-findings.dot", Line = 19 }, { Destination = "scenario-required-inconclusive.dot", Line = 33 }, { Destination = "scenario-config-failure.dot", Line = 48 }, { Destination = "scenario-emit-failure.dot", Line = 63 }, { Destination = "scenario-repeat.dot", Line = 78 }], path = "specs/scenarios.md", sha256 = "6401daf19949f3bce95a9fae651fa01bdc24b7cd3209045f923cae893133ef9f" }
put document doc.286a831ef3ddee95865e5522 { anchors = { vet-pipeline = 1 }, content = "# Vet pipeline\n\n[DOT source](vet.dot)\n\nSpecdot resolves or discovers the manifest, loads a strict configuration,\nselects a profile, and discovers the complete module before parsing any\nrequested entry point. Syntax diagnostics do not abort compilation: they join\nsemantic and finite-check diagnostics in the sealed result.\n\nConfiguration, discovery, sealing, emission, and reporting failures terminate\nwith process status 2. A successfully rendered report terminates with status 1\nwhen errors remain and status 0 otherwise. IR emission occurs before reporting,\nand selecting `--emit-ir -` moves the report to stderr.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: graph.specdot.vet\nkind: algorithm\nsummary: Complete orchestration and process outcomes for specdot vet.\n```\n", links = [{ Destination = "vet.dot", Line = 3 }], path = "specs/vet.md", sha256 = "a6de52a3da4d2e0b75a3ae3d95e325bcdecd997496da9a99bae40aaef6d95a90" }
put document doc.67214831eab57d5fb23de0a6 { anchors = { interfaces = 1, specdot-agent = 22 }, content = "# Interfaces\n\n[DOT source](interfaces.dot)\n\nAn operator autonomously offers one validation request. Specdot separately\naccepts that request, evaluates the complete configured module, and offers one\nresult. The result includes the process outcome and any diagnostics; accepting\nthe result does not imply that the operator agrees with its findings.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: graph.specdot.interfaces\nkind: promise\nsummary: Operator-facing request and result promises for one vet invocation.\n```\n\nThe same operator may independently offer an implementation-conformance\nrequest and accept its result. Conformance acceptance authorizes execution of\nthe trusted repository's configured build and test commands; it does not grant\nthe implementation authority to edit specifications or accept a baseline.\n\n## Specdot agent\n\nThe Specdot process controls its generated IR and report outputs. Reading a\nmodule or probing a remote contract is a capability, not authority over the\nsource or remote content.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: agent.specdot\nkind: agent\nsummary: One autonomous Specdot validation process.\nagent:\n  controls:\n    - resource.ir.output\n    - resource.report.stream\n  capabilities:\n    - promise.specdot.request.accept\n    - promise.specdot.result.offer\n    - promise.specdot.conform.request.accept\n    - promise.specdot.conform.result.offer\n    - effect.specdot.read.module\n    - effect.specdot.read.implementation\n    - effect.specdot.execute.implementation\n    - effect.specdot.probe.remote\n    - effect.specdot.replace.ir\n    - effect.specdot.replace.baseline\n    - effect.specdot.write.report\n  machines:\n    - machine.specdot.vet\n```\n", links = [{ Destination = "interfaces.dot", Line = 3 }], path = "specs/interfaces.md", sha256 = "657d0fa8534ac4b5f86bd2a252670414868fcd724624995e7fecdec9620f8652" }
put document doc.67660134ad3c316d316f2a9b { anchors = { conformance-values = 166, data-model = 1, invocation-boundary = 133, parsed-and-canonical-values = 16, resources-and-identity = 263 }, content = "# Data model\n\n[DOT source](data.dot)\n\nThe public request and result are nominal values. Parsed syntax, canonical IR,\ndiagnostics, and finite-check results remain distinct even when their Go\nrepresentations share primitive encodings.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: graph.specdot.data\nkind: data\nsummary: Nominal inputs, intermediate representations, diagnostics, and output resources.\n```\n\n## Parsed and canonical values\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.source\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: path, type: type.filesystem.path}\n    - {name: kind, type: string}\n    - {name: sha256, type: string}\n  encoding: process-memory\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.parsed.module\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: source_count, type: int}\n    - {name: graph_count, type: int}\n    - {name: contract_count, type: int}\n    - {name: diagnostic_count, type: int}\n  encoding: process-memory\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.ir.graph\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: id, type: string}\n    - {name: kind, type: string}\n    - {name: status, type: string}\n  encoding: json\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.ir.contract\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: for_id, type: string}\n    - {name: kind, type: string}\n    - {name: status, type: string}\n  encoding: json\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.ir.binding\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: offer, type: string}\n    - {name: acceptance, type: string}\n    - {name: body_type, type: string}\n  encoding: json\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.diagnostic\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: rule, type: string}\n    - {name: severity, type: string}\n    - {name: message, type: string}\n    - {name: file, type: type.filesystem.path, optional: true}\n  encoding: process-memory\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.check.run\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: machine, type: string}\n    - {name: status, type: string}\n    - {name: states, type: int}\n    - {name: steps, type: int}\n  encoding: process-memory\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.canonical.ir\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: schema, type: string}\n    - {name: module, type: string}\n    - {name: profile, type: string}\n    - {name: sources, type: type.source.list}\n    - {name: graphs, type: type.ir.graph.list}\n    - {name: contracts, type: type.ir.contract.list}\n    - {name: bindings, type: type.ir.binding.list}\n    - {name: checks, type: type.check.run.list}\n    - {name: digest, type: string}\n  encoding: json\n  version: specdot.ir/v1\n  migration: explicit-version-upgrade\n```\n\n## Invocation boundary\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.vet.request\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: config_path, type: type.filesystem.path, optional: true}\n    - {name: profile, type: string}\n    - {name: format, type: type.report.format}\n    - {name: online, type: bool}\n    - {name: emit_ir, type: type.filesystem.path, optional: true}\n    - {name: paths, type: type.filesystem.path.list}\n  encoding: process-memory\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.vet.result\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: exit_code, type: type.exit.code}\n    - {name: format, type: type.report.format}\n    - {name: digest, type: string, optional: true}\n    - {name: diagnostics, type: type.diagnostic.list}\n    - {name: ir_written, type: bool}\n  encoding: process-memory\n```\n\n## Conformance values\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.implementation.sidecar\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: schema, type: string}\n    - {name: name, type: string}\n    - {name: bindings, type: string}\n    - {name: scenarios, type: string}\n    - {name: coverage, type: string}\n  encoding: yaml\n  version: specdot.implementation/v1\n  migration: explicit-version-upgrade\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.conformance.trace.event\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: scenario_id, type: string}\n    - {name: spec_id, type: string}\n    - {name: phase, type: string, optional: true}\n    - {name: outcome, type: string, optional: true}\n    - {name: sequence, type: int}\n  encoding: ndjson\n  version: specdot.trace/v1\n  migration: explicit-version-upgrade\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.conformance.report\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: implementation, type: string}\n    - {name: spec_digest, type: string}\n    - {name: evidence, type: string}\n    - {name: coverage, type: string}\n    - {name: diagnostics, type: type.diagnostic.list}\n  encoding: json\n  version: specdot.conformance/v1\n  migration: explicit-version-upgrade\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.conformance.baseline\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: implementation, type: string}\n    - {name: spec_digest, type: string}\n    - {name: evidence, type: string}\n    - {name: exclusions, type: string}\n  encoding: json\n  version: specdot.conformance-baseline/v1\n  migration: explicit-version-upgrade\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.conform.request\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: config_path, type: type.filesystem.path, optional: true}\n    - {name: implementation_path, type: type.filesystem.path, optional: true}\n    - {name: profile, type: string}\n    - {name: format, type: type.report.format}\n    - {name: emit_baseline, type: type.filesystem.path, optional: true}\n  encoding: process-memory\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: type.conform.result\nkind: type\ndata:\n  type: record\n  fields:\n    - {name: exit_code, type: type.exit.code}\n    - {name: report, type: type.conformance.report}\n    - {name: baseline_written, type: bool}\n  encoding: process-memory\n```\n\n## Resources and identity\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: resource.spec.module\nkind: resource\ndata:\n  type: resource\n  fields:\n    - {name: manifest, type: type.filesystem.path}\n    - {name: sources, type: type.source.list}\n  mutable: false\n  lifecycle: [discovered, loaded, parsed]\n  encoding: dot-and-markdown\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: resource.remote.markdown\nkind: resource\ndata:\n  type: resource\n  fields:\n    - {name: url, type: string}\n  mutable: false\n  lifecycle: [declared, probed, accepted, rejected]\n  encoding: markdown\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: resource.ir.output\nkind: resource\ndata:\n  type: resource\n  fields:\n    - {name: artifact_id, type: type.artifact.id}\n    - {name: path, type: type.filesystem.path}\n    - {name: digest, type: string}\n  identity: [artifact_id]\n  authority: agent.specdot\n  mutable: true\n  exclusive: true\n  lifecycle: [absent, temporary, sealed, published]\n  retention: [failure, unknown]\n  physical_id_type: type.filesystem.path\n  encoding: json\n  version: specdot.ir/v1\n  migration: explicit-version-upgrade\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: resource.report.stream\nkind: resource\ndata:\n  type: resource\n  fields:\n    - {name: invocation_id, type: type.invocation.id}\n    - {name: format, type: type.report.format}\n  identity: [invocation_id]\n  authority: agent.specdot\n  mutable: true\n  exclusive: true\n  lifecycle: [open, written, closed]\n  encoding: text-json-or-sarif\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: resource.implementation.sidecar\nkind: resource\ndata:\n  type: resource\n  fields:\n    - {name: path, type: type.filesystem.path}\n    - {name: implementation, type: type.implementation.sidecar}\n  mutable: false\n  lifecycle: [declared, loaded, validated]\n  encoding: yaml\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: resource.go.module\nkind: resource\ndata:\n  type: resource\n  fields:\n    - {name: path, type: type.filesystem.path}\n  mutable: false\n  lifecycle: [declared, loaded, type_checked, executed]\n  encoding: go-source\n```\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: resource.conformance.baseline\nkind: resource\ndata:\n  type: resource\n  fields:\n    - {name: artifact_id, type: type.artifact.id}\n    - {name: path, type: type.filesystem.path}\n    - {name: baseline, type: type.conformance.baseline}\n  identity: [artifact_id]\n  authority: agent.specdot\n  mutable: true\n  exclusive: true\n  lifecycle: [absent, candidate, reviewed, replaced]\n  retention: [failure, unknown]\n  physical_id_type: type.filesystem.path\n  encoding: json\n  version: specdot.conformance-baseline/v1\n  migration: explicit-version-upgrade\n```\n", links = [{ Destination = "data.dot", Line = 3 }], path = "specs/data.md", sha256 = "ee5bafc7b911a72dd4651fef74fb6793490e41d83dd6b6f39cad9549783134c6" }
put document doc.92660105db4fe23eb65e8c96 { anchors = { invocation-lifecycle = 1 }, content = "# Invocation lifecycle\n\n[DOT source](lifecycle.dot)\n\nOne process accepts a `Start` event. `Advance` abstracts completion and the\nfilesystem, configuration, validation, and reporting outcomes observed at each\nstage; the implementation remains deterministic for a fixed environment and\nrequest. Each invocation terminates cleanly, with validation findings, or with\na usage/internal failure.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: machine.specdot.vet\nkind: machine\nsummary: Finite lifecycle of one specdot vet process.\nmachine:\n  agent: agent.specdot\n  semantics: run-to-completion\n  events:\n    - name: Start\n      payload: type.vet.request\n      policy: handled\n    - name: Advance\n      policy: handled\nclaims:\n  - id: claim.vet.clean.reachable\n    kind: reachable\n    state: state.vet.clean\n    required: true\n  - id: claim.vet.findings.reachable\n    kind: reachable\n    state: state.vet.findings\n    required: true\n  - id: claim.vet.failure.reachable\n    kind: reachable\n    state: state.vet.failed\n    required: true\n  - id: claim.vet.transition.coverage\n    kind: transition_coverage\n    required: true\n```\n", links = [{ Destination = "lifecycle.dot", Line = 3 }], path = "specs/lifecycle.md", sha256 = "caaa5c02626827751dd9f39838ef1863a91644d0852eb96df742e73f913810f0" }
put document doc.b067cbca148de34fa3aad253 { anchors = { implementation-conformance = 1 }, content = "# Implementation conformance\n\n[DOT source](conform.dot)\n\n`gridspec conform` compares clean accepted specification IR with an existing Go\nimplementation without regenerating or rewriting that implementation.\nLanguage-specific mappings live in a strict `gridspec.implementation/v1`\nsidecar rather than in graph attributes.\n\nStatic bindings resolve stable package objects with Go type information.\nAccepted executable scenario contracts are the primary command oracle. The\nsidecar maps their artifact names to Go packages; it cannot replace their steps\nor expected observations. GridSpec executes them through the same bounded\nrunner used for generated code and records normalized observations in the\nreport. Exact sidecar-bound Go tests and optional deterministic\n`gridspec.trace/v1` events remain supplemental. The deprecated sidecar command\nform is migration-only and is rejected when the same scenario has an accepted\nexecutable contract.\n\nEvery active semantic ID has at least one evidence class or an explicit\nreviewed exclusion. A checked-in baseline preserves the evidence class\npreviously established for each ID. Coverage floors and an exclusion limit\nprevent new specification surface from silently weakening the comparison.\nBaseline emission is explicit and produces a review candidate; a normal run\nnever blesses regressions.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: graph.specdot.conform\nkind: algorithm\nsummary: Deterministically compare a vetted specification with existing Go code.\nmetadata:\n  implementation_schema: gridspec.implementation/v1\n  report_schema: gridspec.conformance/v1\n  trace_schema: gridspec.trace/v1\n  baseline_schema: gridspec.conformance-baseline/v1\n  command_authority: accepted executable scenario contracts\n  shared_runner: generated and existing Go use the same accepted-scenario runner\n  sidecar_role: artifact and symbol mappings plus supplemental test and trace evidence\n```\n", links = [{ Destination = "conform.dot", Line = 3 }], path = "specs/conform.md", sha256 = "ff32a2308ac9b84d74b428aa739c2f3838948dead2151f5917852679201c132b" }
put document doc.b5b6623f372c6bab865fe705 { anchors = { finite-checker = 1 }, content = "# Finite checker\n\n[DOT source](checker.dot)\n\nThe native checker canonicalizes configurations and explores them breadth first\nwithin the selected finite bounds. Safety violations retain a shortest witness.\nAfter reachable-state exploration, strongly connected components evaluate\nliveness claims. Unsupported history semantics or exhausted bounds are\ninconclusive rather than silently successful.\n\nEvery run is classified as `passed`, `violated`, `inconclusive`, `assumed`, or\n`unchecked`. A profile with required finite checking converts inconclusive or\nunchecked required work into a vet error.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: graph.specdot.checker\nkind: algorithm\nsummary: Bounded safety and liveness analysis over canonical machine configurations.\n```\n", links = [{ Destination = "checker.dot", Line = 3 }], path = "specs/checker.md", sha256 = "492d5f06666898064ea9c445c680766c894c0e5700bba2af05442795fbb2dcb5" }
put document doc.ce196fbe671845a45be55b8a { anchors = { side-effects = 1 }, content = "# Side effects\n\n[DOT source](effects.dot)\n\nSource reads and remote probes do not grant Specdot authority over their\ncontent. Report publication is controlled by the current process. IR emission\nwrites a temporary file, synchronizes it, closes it, and renames it over the\ntarget; failure or uncertainty retains the previously published target.\n\nConformance additionally reads a trusted implementation sidecar, invokes Go\nbuilds, tests, and configured binaries, and may explicitly publish a baseline\ncandidate. Execution is never implied by `vet`; it occurs only under the\n`conform` command. Baseline replacement uses the same atomic publication and\nfailure-retention policy as canonical IR emission.\n\n```specdot\nschema: specdot.contract/v1\nfor: graph.specdot.effects\nkind: decision\nsummary: Filesystem, network, report, and canonical-IR effects of one vet invocation.\n```\n", links = [{ Destination = "effects.dot", Line = 3 }], path = "specs/effects.md", sha256 = "647a0cd69bd09ccc01e578b39a06eb12555131562b47a5993e1fc8cf74e55540" }
put document doc.gridspec.architecture { anchors = { authority-model = 3, cas-materialization-boundary = 18, compiler-and-disassembler = 58, gridspec-architecture = 1, llm-boundary = 209, shared-executable-qualification = 84, signatures-and-git-carriers = 155, what-is-and-is-not-provable = 186 }, content = "# GridSpec architecture\n\n## Authority model\n\nGridSpec treats specifications as source code. The authoritative state is an\nimmutable Grid-CBOR snapshot reached by replaying finite GridSpec transactions.\nDOT and prose are generated views; legacy DOT and Markdown are migration inputs.\nGo is compiler output or an implementation being checked against the specs.\n\nA transaction event binds the exact program bytes, base state, result state,\nrequired links, and previous event. An event can be an unsigned tool/LLM\nproposal or a signed independently authored transaction. Neither is accepted\nmerely because it exists. Accepted state is the `main` head selected by a\ncryptographically signed Grid Commit v3 containing the complete timeline-head\nmap. This separation lets tools propose aggressively without granting them\nauthority.\n\n## CAS materialization boundary\n\nThe content-addressed state is part of compilation, not an optional archive\nwritten after compilation. GridSpec transaction text describes a change to a\nbase state; by itself it is not the complete specification. The compiler first\nparses and replays the transaction, materializes the complete result closure in\nCAS, and verifies the resulting state CID. Every downstream operation consumes\nthat CID:\n\n```text\ntransaction bytes + base state CID\n              -> deterministic replay\n              -> materialized result state CID in CAS\n              -> vet\n              -> proposal or signed acceptance\n              -> text/DOT views, conformance, documentation, and Go generation\n```\n\nThere is no raw-transaction-to-LLM generation path. A generator reloads and\nrehashes the selected state closure from CAS before rendering its model input.\nAccepted state is the default compiler input. An operator may explicitly build\nor inspect a current vetted proposal, but the proposal is still a materialized\nstate CID and remains distinct from signed acceptance.\n\nThe model is similar to Dockerfiles and image storage:\n\n| Docker concept | GridSpec concept |\n| --- | --- |\n| Dockerfile or build instruction | GridSpec transaction program |\n| base image digest | base state CID |\n| layer transition and resulting image | transaction event and result state CID |\n| local content store or registry | Git-backed Grid CAS |\n| signed release manifest or digest-pinned tag | signed Grid Commit selecting timeline heads |\n| consumer using `FROM ...@sha256` | view, checker, or generator using a state CID |\n\nThis is an analogy, not a wire-format claim. OCI layers are filesystem deltas;\nGridSpec transactions are semantic transitions and the result is an\nindependently verified semantic snapshot. Git commits carry the blocks, but the\nGrid CIDs—not Git object IDs or mutable refs—identify compiler inputs.\n\n## Compiler and disassembler\n\nThe forward path is:\n\n```text\naccepted or explicitly selected proposal state CID -> verify CAS closure\n               -> derived semantic IR -> LLM translation -> candidate Go\n               -> deterministic shape checks -> gofmt -> staged module\n               -> generated Go tests -> accepted command scenarios\n               -> qualified output and CAS evidence -> review\n```\n\nThe reverse path is:\n\n```text\naccepted state CID + existing Go -> deterministic source bundle\n            -> LLM reconstruction\n            -> parsed GridSpec transaction -> replay -> vet -> proposal\n```\n\nAn LLM is therefore part of both the compiler and disassembler. Its output is\nnot merely commentary, but it is also not accepted on trust. The deterministic\nshell checks syntax, paths, ownership, content hashes, state replay, semantic\nrules, bounded models, and executable tests. A human or separately authorized\nagent accepts the resulting transaction or code change.\n\n## Shared executable qualification\n\nAccepted specifications contain language-neutral `contract.scenario` command\noracles. Each names an artifact, covered semantic IDs, inline fixture files, a\nrepeat count, and ordered process steps with exact exit, stream, and file\nexpectations. Generated-file responses and implementation sidecars separately\nmap those artifact names to Go main packages. A package mapping selects code;\nit cannot redefine accepted behavior. Model-authored Go tests and sidecar-bound\nGo tests remain supplemental evidence. Generated artifact mappings may name\nonly `.` or a `./`-relative package inside the staged module.\n\n`build` writes a temporary module, runs its Go tests, builds the mapped\nartifacts, and executes every accepted scenario. Each repeat receives a fresh\nfixture directory; steps within a repeat are separate processes sharing that\ndirectory. This exposes persisted state and restart behavior. GridSpec rejects\ntimeouts, oversized output, mismatched observations, and filesystem changes a\nstep did not declare. Only a passing stage is renamed into the requested output\ntree.\n\n`update` uses the same gate. Even a dry run reconstructs the existing Go bundle\nin a temporary module, applies the proposed owned-file changes there, and\nqualifies that result without touching the source tree. Write mode rechecks the\nsource hashes after qualification and applies only the already-tested bytes,\nwith rollback protection for write failures.\n\n`conform` applies the same accepted-scenario runner to hand-written Go without\nregenerating or rewriting it. It builds each mapped artifact with workspace\nmode disabled and module files read-only, then uses the same fresh fixtures,\nordered processes, limits, normalization, and exact comparisons as `build` and\n`update`. The sidecar remains authoritative for artifact and symbol mappings,\ncoverage policy, and legacy Go-test or trace bindings. Its older command form\nis accepted only for scenario IDs without an executable accepted contract; a\nduplicate sidecar command oracle is an error.\n\nThe deterministic `gridspec.qualification/v1` report binds source identity,\noutput digest, artifact mappings, Go-test observations, normalized scenario\nobservations, and expected-file hashes. It is stored as the verification bytes\nalready linked by the derived-artifact v1 CAS message. The ownership manifest\nv3 records the corresponding evidence digest and scenario IDs; v1 and v2\nmanifests remain readable.\n\nExisting-code conformance stores the same normalized scenario observations in\nits `gridspec.conformance/v1` report, then combines their covered IDs with\nstatic, Go-test, trace, exclusion, floor, and baseline evidence. Ordinary\nconformance is read-only unless the operator explicitly records the report as\nan unsigned candidate.\n\nWhen no executable scenario is accepted, compilation does not degrade to a\nwarning or unsafe mode. `gridspec scenarios` asks an LLM for bounded strict JSON,\nconstructs a preserving transaction itself, stores the exact exchange in CAS,\nand publishes an unsigned proposal. `build` and `update` can initiate that same\nproposal path, but emit no code until a separate signed acceptance. A proposal\npreview never creates a proposal on top of itself.\n\n`gridspec diff --proposal CID` recursively compares the two materialized states\nand emits sorted JSON-pointer changes. It is deterministic review machinery;\nit does not ask an LLM to decide whether the change is acceptable.\n\nCompilation and disassembly are partial and potentially lossy. A specification\ncan intentionally leave implementation latitude; Go can contain optimizations,\nincidental structure, or behavior not recoverable as design intent. Round trips\nneed not reproduce identical text:\n\n```text\ndisassemble(compile(spec)) ~= spec\ncompile(disassemble(go))   ~= go\n```\n\nHere `~=` means agreement on explicitly selected observations and invariants,\nnot byte identity or unrestricted semantic equivalence.\n\n## Signatures and Git carriers\n\nGridSpec stores content-addressed Grid blocks as Git blobs under\n`blocks/<CID-prefix>/<CID-rest>`. A carrier tree has a `ROOT` file containing\nthe root CID. Git commit IDs provide transport, reachability, atomic ref updates,\nand compatibility with fetch/push; they are not GridSpec semantic identities.\nEvery block is rehashed against its CID when read.\n\nCAS therefore serves the same two roles during local compilation and transport:\nit is the verified local compiler-input store, and its Git carriers make those\ninputs reachable elsewhere. Generation never bypasses the local CAS merely\nbecause all transaction text happens to be available in the working tree.\n\nAccepted carriers use:\n\n```text\nrefs/gridspec/projects/<encoded-project>/<agent-CID>\n```\n\nProposals use `refs/gridspec/proposals/...`; fetched refs are isolated below\n`refs/gridspec/remotes/...`. Publication uses compare-and-swap ref updates so\nconcurrent acceptance cannot silently overwrite another head.\nThe signing key is generated with mode `0600` below the repository's Git\ndirectory, configured by path, and excluded from carrier closures. LLM requests\nreceive specification or source bytes, never the key.\n\nThe Grid Commit v3 signature covers the protocol message, predecessor list,\ncomplete timeline selection, metadata root, agent identity, algorithm, and\npublic key. Receiver-local policy binds that public key to an agent identity;\ncryptographic validity alone does not establish trust.\n\n## What is and is not provable\n\nNothing here violates the halting problem, Rice's theorem, or undecidability of\ngeneral program equivalence. GridSpec does not promise a total, semantics-\npreserving compiler/disassembler pair for arbitrary Go programs. It makes\nsmaller claims with explicit boundaries:\n\n- the transaction language has no loops, recursion, I/O, clocks, randomness,\n  or process execution, so replay terminates in steps bounded by program size;\n- Grid-CBOR encoding, CIDs, reference sets, signatures, and replay are exact;\n- finite-state checks are conclusive only within their declared bounds and\n  report `inconclusive` when limits prevent a proof;\n- conformance combines static bindings, accepted command contracts, Go tests,\n  semantic traces, coverage floors, exclusions, and reviewed baselines;\n- generated Go is accepted only after deterministic validation and tests, which\n  demonstrate selected behavior rather than proving all behavior;\n- disassembly produces a hypothesis/proposal whose omissions and uncertainty\n  must remain reviewable.\n\nThe impossible claim would be “for every Go program, automatically recover a\ncomplete specification and prove the regenerated program equivalent.” GridSpec\ndoes not make it.\n\n## LLM boundary\n\nLLMs are active transformation engines for code-to-spec, spec/IR-to-code,\nrepair suggestions, explanatory views, and other semantic translations. Calling\nthem “advisory” understates their role. The correct boundary is capability and\nauthority:\n\n- they may create complete candidate transactions and complete candidate code;\n- they may propose bounded graph, node, and edge descriptions, while the tool\n  constructs field-preserving transactions and stores request/response audit\n  data in CAS;\n- their raw output is untrusted input to deterministic parsers and checkers;\n- provider/model/response identity is recorded as provenance where useful;\n- a model failure cannot mutate accepted state;\n- a model never receives signing material or calls the acceptance operation;\n- acceptance is a separate signed transaction-selection act.\n\nHuman-readable titles and summaries are part of accepted state. DOT renderers\nnever ask a model to invent labels: they use IDs by default and may format any\nstored entity field explicitly. This keeps identical state CIDs reproducible\nacross machines and makes descriptive changes reviewable like code changes.\n\nSee the frozen wire descriptions in [`protocols/`](../protocols/) and the\ntransaction grammar in\n[`transaction-language-v1.md`](../protocols/transaction-language-v1.md).\n", links = [{ Destination = "../protocols/", Line = 231 }, { Destination = "../protocols/transaction-language-v1.md", Line = 233 }], path = "docs/architecture.md", sha256 = "16a285eb3c95fd709a8f727eb5dd12c0254a7dfc02f11789e32940c92c6ca275" }
put edge decision.edge.bootstrap.conformance { attrs = { specdot_branch = "current-deterministic-conformance", specdot_id = "decision.edge.bootstrap.conformance", specdot_kind = "decision" }, element = "edge", from = "decision.bootstrap.authority", graph_id = "decision.specdot.bootstrap", id = "decision.edge.bootstrap.conformance", kind = "decision", location = { column = 41, file = "specs/architecture.dot", line = 12 }, status = "active", summary = "Extends bootstrap authority with deterministic conformance evidence without treating self-checking as proof.", title = "Add conformance evidence", to = "decision.bootstrap.conformance" }
